# Module-level ans_49_150
ans_49_150 = {
    49: ('A', 'Neisseria meningitidis is the leading cause of bacterial meningitis in young adults living in close quarters (college dorms) and is characteristically associated with a petechial/purpuric rash. CSF demonstrates marked neutrophilic pleocytosis, markedly elevated protein, and low glucose.'),
    50: ('D', 'Listeria monocytogenes is a facultative intracellular gram-positive rod with characteristic tumbling motility that causes meningitis in immunocompromised patients, neonates, and older adults (>50 years). Ampicillin must be added empirically because Listeria is intrinsically resistant to all cephalosporins.'),
    51: ('C', 'Herpes simplex virus (HSV-1) encephalitis is a neurological emergency presenting with fever, altered mental status, and frontotemporal deficits. Because untreated mortality exceeds 70%, immediate empiric IV acyclovir must be initiated promptly without waiting for confirmatory CSF PCR results.'),
    52: ('B', 'HSV-1 has a strong predilection for the inferomedial temporal and inferior frontal lobes. Necrosis of the temporal lobe damages Meyer loop (producing contralateral superior quadrantanopia / "pie-in-the-sky"), the uncus (olfactory hallucinations), and the hippocampus (severe anterograde memory impairment).'),
    53: ('C', 'Rabies virus reaches the CNS via retrograde axonal transport along peripheral nerves following an animal bite (raccoons, bats). Clinical manifestations include agitation, autonomic dysfunction, hydrophobia, and pharyngeal spasms. Histopathology shows pathognomonic Negri bodies (eosinophilic intracytoplasmic inclusions) in cerebellar Purkinje cells and hippocampal pyramidal neurons.'),
    54: ('A', 'Varicella-zoster virus (VZV) establishes life-long latency in sensory ganglia (dorsal root ganglia and cranial nerve sensory ganglia such as the trigeminal ganglion) following primary chickenpox infection. Reactivation presents as herpes zoster (shingles) in a dermatomal distribution.'),
    55: ('C', 'In patients with HIV and CD4 < 100/µL, multiple ring-enhancing lesions in the basal ganglia with positive Toxoplasma serology is classic for Toxoplasma gondii encephalitis. First-line therapy is oral pyrimethamine and sulfadiazine along with leucovorin (folinic acid).'),
    56: ('D', 'Primary central nervous system lymphoma (PCNSL) is an aggressive diffuse large B-cell lymphoma strongly associated with Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) in severely immunocompromised patients (CD4 < 50/µL). It presents as a solitary periventricular enhancing mass with subependymal spread. Detection of EBV DNA by PCR in the CSF is highly sensitive and specific.'),
    57: ('E', 'Cryptococcal meningitis (Cryptococcus neoformans) presents with subacute headache and markedly elevated opening pressure (>25 cm H2O). Initial induction therapy is IV amphotericin B plus flucytosine for at least 2 weeks, combined with serial therapeutic lumbar punctures to manage intracranial hypertension, followed by oral fluconazole maintenance.'),
    58: ('B', 'Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML) is caused by reactivation of the JC polyomavirus in immunocompromised states, notably patients on natalizumab (anti-alpha-4 integrin therapy). The virus selectively infects and destroys oligodendrocytes, causing demyelination presenting as non-enhancing subcortical white matter lesions on MRI without mass effect.'),
    59: ('C', 'Cytomegalovirus (CMV) lumbosacral polyradiculopathy / radiculitis is an opportunistic infection in advanced AIDS (CD4 < 50/µL) causing rapidly progressive flaccid lower extremity weakness, saddle sensory loss, urinary retention, and severe radicular pain. CSF shows a characteristic neutrophilic pleocytosis with elevated protein and hypoglycorrhachia.'),
    60: ('A', 'HIV-associated neurocognitive disorder (HAND) is driven by chronic neuroinflammation from direct viral replication in perivascular macrophages and microglia. MRI shows diffuse cerebral cortical atrophy and symmetric, confluent subcortical white matter T2/FLAIR hyperintensities without mass effect or focal enhancement.'),
    61: ('E', 'Immune Reconstitution Inflammatory Syndrome (IRIS) occurs after starting antiretroviral therapy (ART) in patients with opportunistic infections (e.g., Cryptococcus). As the immune system recovers (CD4 count increases and viral load plummets), an exuberant paradoxical inflammatory response produces worsening clinical symptoms and elevated intracranial pressure.'),
    62: ('D', 'Infective endocarditis in people who inject drugs (frequently involving the tricuspid or aortic valves) can generate septic emboli that lodge in distal arterial branches at the gray-white junction, producing multiple cerebral microabscesses. Staphylococcus aureus is the most common causative organism.'),
    63: ('B', 'Naegleria fowleri is a free-living freshwater amoeba that causes rapidly fatal primary amoebic meningoencephalitis (PAM). It penetrates the cribriform plate after entering nasal passages during warm freshwater swimming and migrates along olfactory nerve fibers into the frontal lobes.'),
    64: ('B', 'The anterior cerebral artery (ACA) supplies the medial surface of the frontal and parietal lobes, including the medial motor and sensory strip (paracentral lobule). Infarction causes contralateral weakness and sensory loss predominantly affecting the leg and foot, relative sparing of the arm and face, and urinary incontinence.'),
    65: ('C', 'The superior division of the middle cerebral artery (MCA) supplies the lateral precentral gyrus, postcentral gyrus, and inferior frontal gyrus (Broca area). An infarct causes contralateral hemiparesis predominantly affecting the face and arm with expressive (Broca) non-fluent aphasia, while comprehension remains preserved.'),
    66: ('D', 'The inferior division of the left middle cerebral artery supplies the superior temporal gyrus (Wernicke area) and visual radiations. Occlusion causes Wernicke aphasia (fluent, effortless speech full of paraphasias and neologisms, with severely impaired comprehension and repetition) and a contralateral superior quadrantanopia.'),
    67: ('E', 'Conduction aphasia is caused by damage to the arcuate fasciculus (the white matter tract connecting Wernicke and Broca areas). Patients have fluent grammatical speech and intact auditory comprehension, but repetition is severely impaired (e.g., unable to repeat "no ifs, ands, or buts").'),
    68: ('A', 'Hemispatial neglect syndrome results from an ischemic lesion of the non-dominant (typically right) posterior parietal cortex / inferior parietal lobule, supplied by the right middle cerebral artery (MCA). Patients typically neglect the contralateral left hemispace, ignoring stimuli, objects, and their own body on the left side.'),
    69: ('E', 'The posterior cerebral artery (PCA) supplies the primary visual cortex (calcarine sulcus) in the occipital lobe. Infarction causes a contralateral homonymous hemianopia. Macular vision is typically spared because the occipital pole (macular representation) receives dual collateral perfusion from the middle cerebral artery.'),
    70: ('D', 'Pure motor hemiparesis is the most common lacunar stroke syndrome, typically resulting from infarction of the posterior limb of the internal capsule due to lipohyalinosis or microatheroma of the deep penetrating lenticulostriate branches of the middle cerebral artery.'),
    71: ('C', 'The anterior choroidal artery arises from the internal carotid artery and supplies the posterior limb of the internal capsule, optic tract / lateral geniculate body, and globus pallidus. Occlusion produces the classic "triple H" triad: contralateral hemiparesis, contralateral hemisensory loss, and contralateral homonymous hemianopia.'),
    72: ('A', 'Saccular aneurysms at the junction of the internal carotid artery and posterior communicating artery (ICA-PCoA) compress the adjacent oculomotor nerve (CN III). The parasympathetic pupilloconstrictor fibers travel superficially on the exterior surface of the nerve and are compressed first, causing pupillary dilation (mydriasis) before somatic motor fibers are paralyzed.'),
    73: ('C', 'Severe systemic hypotension causes border-zone (watershed) infarcts at the hemodynamic junctions between major cerebral territories. Bilateral anterior watershed infarcts (between ACA and MCA) involve the proximal motor cortex, producing the "man-in-a-barrel" syndrome: proximal shoulder and hip weakness with preserved distal hand and foot function.'),
    74: ('B', 'Cerebral venous sinus thrombosis (CVST) often presents postpartum with headache, signs of raised ICP, seizures, and venous hemorrhagic infarction. First-line medical therapy is full-dose systemic intravenous heparin anticoagulation, which halts propagation of the venous clot and facilitates recanalization, even when secondary hemorrhage is present.'),
    75: ('E', 'The abducens nerve (CN VI) runs freely through the center of the cavernous sinus lumen in close proximity to the internal carotid artery. Cranial nerves III, IV, V1, and V2 are embedded within the fibrous lateral dural wall of the sinus. In cavernous sinus thrombosis, CN VI is often involved early, producing lateral rectus palsy.'),
    76: ('A', 'Saccular (berry) aneurysms most frequently occur at arterial branch points in the circle of Willis. The anterior communicating artery (ACom) junction is the single most common site (accounting for ~40% of all saccular aneurysms), followed by the posterior communicating artery (PCoA) and middle cerebral artery (MCA) bifurcations.'),
    77: ('C', 'Chronic systemic hypertension induces lipohyalinosis and Charcot-Bouchard microaneurysms in small deep penetrating lenticulostriate arteries. Rupture of these microaneurysms leads to hypertensive intracerebral hemorrhage, most commonly located in the putamen/basal ganglia, followed by thalamus, pons, and cerebellum.'),
    78: ('D', 'The intracranial dural venous drainage follows a direct anatomical path: the superior sagittal sinus flows into the confluence of sinuses (torcular Herophili), which drains laterally into the transverse sinuses, continues into the sigmoid sinuses, and exits through the jugular foramen as the internal jugular veins.'),
    79: ('B', 'An emergent noncontrast head CT is the essential first diagnostic study in suspected acute ischemic stroke. It immediately differentiates acute ischemic stroke from intracranial hemorrhage, which is an absolute contraindication to intravenous thrombolysis.'),
    80: ('D', 'Intravenous alteplase (tPA) is indicated for acute ischemic stroke when administered within 4.5 hours of symptom onset in patients with no active hemorrhage, blood pressure < 185/110 mm Hg, normal coagulation parameters (INR <= 1.7), and no recent major surgery.'),
    81: ('E', 'In patients with large vessel occlusions (LVO) of the anterior circulation (e.g., MCA M1 segment) presenting beyond the IV thrombolysis window (up to 24 hours) who demonstrate a small ischemic core and substantial salvageable penumbra on CT/MR perfusion, endovascular mechanical thrombectomy is first-line therapy.'),
    82: ('A', 'In cerebral ischemia, cellular ATP depletion impairs the Na+/K+-ATPase pump, producing membrane depolarization. Depolarization triggers excessive vesicular glutamate release and impairs glutamate reuptake. Sustained glutamate activates postsynaptic NMDA and AMPA receptors, driving massive calcium influx that activates destructive proteases and lipases.'),
    83: ('C', 'Global cerebral ischemia selectively damages the most metabolically active and vulnerable neuronal populations: the CA1 pyramidal neurons of the hippocampus (Sommer sector), Purkinje cells of the cerebellum, and cortical pyramidal neurons in neocortical layers III, V, and VI (pseudolaminar necrosis).'),
    84: ('E', 'At day 8 post-infarction, the tissue undergoes subacute liquefactive necrosis characterized by abundant lipid-laden foamy macrophages (microglia/monocytes) clearing myelin and cellular debris, robust capillary neovascularization, and early reactive gemistocytic astrogliosis at the infarct margins.'),
    85: ('C', 'In nonvalvular atrial fibrillation, disorganization of atrial contraction leads to blood stasis, especially within the left atrial appendage (LAA). Thrombi formed in the LAA can embolize through the aorta into the carotid and cerebral circulation, causing large territorial strokes.'),
    86: ('D', 'Extracranial internal carotid artery (ICA) dissection occurs when an intimal tear creates an intramural hematoma. The expanded artery stretches and compresses the ascending sympathetic nerve plexus traveling along the carotid adventitia, resulting in an ipsilateral postganglionic Horner syndrome (partial: ptosis and miosis without facial anhidrosis).'),
    87: ('B', 'In patients with symptomatic high-grade (70-99%) internal carotid artery stenosis presenting with TIA or nondisabling stroke, carotid endarterectomy (CEA) combined with optimal medical therapy (antiplatelets and statins) provides superior long-term secondary stroke prevention compared to medical management alone.'),
    88: ('A', 'Pure motor lacunar stroke results from occlusion of small deep penetrating end-arteries (lenticulostriate branches of MCA). The underlying vascular pathology is lipohyalinosis and microatheroma driven by chronic hypertension and diabetes mellitus, producing focal cavitary lacunes in the internal capsule.'),
    89: ('C', 'In suspected subarachnoid hemorrhage (SAH) where noncontrast head CT is negative (especially >6 hours after onset), a lumbar puncture is required. Finding xanthochromia (yellow CSF supernatant from enzymatic hemoglobin breakdown to bilirubin) with persistent RBC counts across serial tubes confirms SAH and rules out a traumatic tap.'),
    90: ('D', 'Delayed cerebral ischemia due to cerebral vasospasm typically occurs 4 to 14 days following aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage. Enteral nimodipine (a dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker) is administered routinely to all patients with aneurysmal SAH to prevent vasospasm and improve neurological outcomes.'),
    91: ('B', 'In life-threatening warfarin-associated intracranial hemorrhage, emergent reversal of anticoagulation is achieved using four-factor prothrombin complex concentrate (4F-PCC), which rapidly restores clotting factors II, VII, IX, and X, administered in conjunction with intravenous vitamin K to sustain synthesis of new coagulation factors.'),
    92: ('A', 'Therapeutic hyperventilation lowers arterial partial pressure of carbon dioxide (PaCO2 < 35 mm Hg), which induces cerebral arteriolar vasoconstriction. This leads to an acute decrease in cerebral blood volume (CBV) and a rapid reduction in intracranial pressure (ICP) as an emergency temporizing measure.'),
    93: ('C', 'Cold-water caloric irrigation of the external auditory canal stimulates the vestibulo-ocular reflex (VOR). In a comatose patient, absence of ocular deviation indicates severe structural destruction or depression of brainstem circuits in the pontine tegmentum (vestibular nuclei, abducens nuclei, and MLF).'),
    94: ('B', 'Weber syndrome is an alternating hemiplegia caused by infarction of the ventromedial rostral midbrain (PCA paramedian branches). It interrupts the emerging CN III fascicles (producing ipsilateral third nerve palsy: ptosis, mydriasis, eye turned "down and out") and the crus cerebri / corticospinal tract (producing contralateral spastic hemiparesis).'),
    95: ('C', 'Benedikt syndrome results from a stroke in the paramedian midbrain tegmentum (PCA territory). It involves the CN III fascicles (ipsilateral oculomotor palsy) and the red nucleus with crossing dentatorubrothalamic fibers of the superior cerebellar peduncle (producing contralateral coarse intention tremor, choreoathetosis, and ataxia).'),
    96: ('E', 'Occlusion of the rostral basilar artery ("top of the basilar" syndrome) compromises blood flow to the bilateral posterior cerebral and superior cerebellar arteries. Ischemia to the bilateral thalami and upper midbrain results in profound somnolence or coma, vertical gaze palsies (Parinaud syndrome), and pupillary abnormalities.'),
    97: ('B', 'Mid-pontine lateral infarction (basilar circumferential branches) involves the trigeminal motor and principal sensory nuclei and the spinothalamic tract. Patients exhibit ipsilateral weakness of the muscles of mastication (jaw deviates toward the side of the lesion), ipsilateral facial hemianesthesia, and contralateral loss of pain and temperature on the body.'),
    98: ('D', 'A caudal medial pontine stroke (Millard-Gubler / Foville variant, supplied by basilar paramedian branches) interrupts the exiting CN VI fascicles (producing ipsilateral lateral rectus palsy with inability to abduct the eye) and the descending corticospinal tract in the basis pontis (producing contralateral spastic hemiparesis).'),
    99: ('D', 'Lateral inferior pontine syndrome is caused by occlusion of the anterior inferior cerebellar artery (AICA). Key distinguishing features include ipsilateral peripheral seventh nerve palsy (complete hemifacial paralysis including the forehead), hearing loss and tinnitus (CN VIII / labyrinthine artery), limb ataxia (middle cerebellar peduncle), and Horner syndrome.'),
    100: ('C', 'Lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome is caused by occlusion of the posterior inferior cerebellar artery (PICA) or vertebral artery. Manifestations include dysphagia and hoarseness (nucleus ambiguus / CN IX, X), vertigo and nystagmus (vestibular nuclei), ipsilateral facial pain/temp loss (spinal V), contralateral body pain/temp loss (spinothalamic tract), ipsilateral Horner syndrome, and limb ataxia.'),
    101: ('E', 'In lateral medullary syndrome, ischemia of the nucleus ambiguus impairs motor innervation of the ipsilateral levator veli palatini (CN X). When the patient says "ah", the intact contralateral muscle lifts the palate normally, causing the uvula to deviate AWAY from the side of the lesion (to the right in a left-sided Wallenberg stroke).'),
    102: ('A', 'Medial medullary (Dejerine) syndrome is caused by occlusion of the anterior spinal artery (ASA). It damages the hypoglossal nerve (CN XII: ipsilateral flaccid tongue paralysis with deviation toward the lesion), the pyramidal tract (contralateral hemiparesis), and the medial lemniscus (contralateral loss of vibration and joint position sense).'),
    103: ('A', 'Locked-in syndrome is caused by basilar artery occlusion producing bilateral infarction of the basis pontis (ventral pons). Bilateral corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts are interrupted, causing total quadriplegia and anarthria. Because the tegmentum, reticular activating system, and CN III/IV are spared, patients remain fully conscious and communicate via vertical eye movements and blinking.'),
    104: ('D', 'The motor neurons of the facial nucleus that innervate the upper face (forehead) receive bilateral corticobulbar projections from both cerebral hemispheres, whereas lower facial motor neurons receive only contralateral input. A unilateral upper motor neuron (corticobulbar) lesion produces weakness of the contralateral lower face with sparing of the forehead.'),
    105: ('A', 'Infarction of the sensory thalamic relay nuclei (ventral posterolateral [VPL] and ventral posteromedial [VPM] nuclei, supplied by thalamogeniculate branches of PCA) causes contralateral hemisensory loss. Weeks to months later, patients may develop severe, intractable neuropathic burning pain (Dejerine-Roussy thalamic pain syndrome).'),
    106: ('D', 'Hemiballismus consists of sudden, violent, involuntary flinging movements of the contralateral limbs. It is caused by a lesion (typically ischemic or hemorrhagic) of the contralateral subthalamic nucleus, which normally provides excitatory glutamatergic drive to the globus pallidus internus to inhibit unwanted motor movements.'),
    107: ('B', 'The descending first-order sympathetic pathway travels through the lateral brainstem (lateral midbrain, lateral pons, and lateral medulla). A medial medullary infarct affects medial structures (pyramid, hypoglossal nerve, medial lemniscus) and characteristically spares the lateral sympathetic tract, so it will not cause Horner syndrome.'),
    108: ('E', 'The vascular supply of the brainstem follows predictable territorial branches: the rostral midbrain is supplied by the posterior cerebral artery (PCA) and superior cerebellar artery (SCA); the pons is supplied by the basilar artery; the lateral medulla by PICA; and the medial medulla by the anterior spinal artery (ASA).'),
    109: ('C', 'Alzheimer disease is the most common cause of neurodegenerative dementia. Pathology typically begins in the transentorhinal cortex and progresses into the entorhinal cortex and hippocampus, resulting in early prominent atrophy of the medial temporal lobes and insidious decline in episodic memory.'),
    110: ('B', 'The gene encoding Amyloid Precursor Protein (APP) is located on chromosome 21. Individuals with Down syndrome (Trisomy 21) possess an extra copy of the APP gene (gene dosage effect), resulting in elevated production of amyloid-beta peptides and early-onset Alzheimer disease pathology by age 40 to 50.'),
    111: ('D', 'Senile (neuritic) plaques in Alzheimer disease consist of extracellular aggregates of amyloid-beta (Aβ42). Aβ42 is generated by the sequential amyloidogenic cleavage of Amyloid Precursor Protein (APP), first by beta-secretase (BACE1) and subsequently by intramembrane gamma-secretase (presenilin complex).'),
    112: ('B', 'Donepezil is a centrally active, reversible acetylcholinesterase inhibitor that increases acetylcholine availability in the cerebral cortex. Enhancing parasympathetic and central cholinergic activity commonly leads to cholinergic side effects, including gastrointestinal distress (nausea, diarrhea), vivid dreams, and bradycardia.'),
    113: ('C', 'Memantine is an uncompetitive, moderate-affinity NMDA receptor antagonist. Pathological chronic mild elevation of extracellular glutamate leads to sustained calcium influx and excitotoxic neuronal death; memantine blocks this tonic pathological activation while preserving normal physiological synaptic transmission.'),
    114: ('E', 'Dementia with Lewy Bodies (DLB) is characterized by fluctuating cognition, recurrent detailed visual hallucinations, spontaneous parkinsonism, and early REM sleep behavior disorder. Neuropathology demonstrates round, eosinophilic intracytoplasmic Lewy bodies composed of aggregated alpha-synuclein in cortical and brainstem neurons.'),
    115: ('D', 'Behavioral variant frontotemporal lobar degeneration (bv-FTLD / Pick disease) presents in younger patients (50s-60s) with profound personality changes, social disinhibition, hyperorality, and apathy with relatively spared memory. Autopsy reveals prominent atrophy of the frontal and anterior temporal lobes with inclusions of hyperphosphorylated tau (Pick bodies) or TDP-43.'),
    116: ('A', 'Creutzfeldt-Jakob Disease (CJD) is a rapidly progressive transmissible spongiform encephalopathy. Its pathogenesis involves the conformational conversion of normal alpha-helical cellular prion protein (PrPC) into the abnormal, protease-resistant beta-sheet scrapie isoform (PrPSc), which induces further template-directed misfolding.'),
    117: ('B', 'Diagnostic hallmarks of sporadic CJD include periodic synchronous sharp-wave complexes (PSWCs) on EEG, elevated 14-3-3 protein and positive real-time quaking-induced conversion (RT-QuIC) in the CSF, and cortical ribboning with basal ganglia hyperintensity on diffusion-weighted MRI (DWI).'),
    118: ('E', 'Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus (NPH) is characterized by the classic clinical triad of magnetic/apraxic gait ("wet, wacky, wobbly"), cognitive decline, and urinary incontinence. Neuroimaging shows ventriculomegaly disproportionate to sulcal enlargement. High-volume CSF removal via LP tap test is diagnostic, and placement of a ventriculoperitoneal (VP) shunt is curative.'),
    119: ('D', 'Vascular dementia is characterized by a stepwise cognitive decline temporally related to discrete cerebrovascular events (strokes) or progressive microvascular subcortical ischemic white matter disease. Management focuses on rigorous secondary prevention and control of vascular risk factors (hypertension, diabetes, lipids, smoking).'),
    120: ('A', 'Huntington disease is an autosomal dominant neurodegenerative disorder caused by an unstable CAG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the HTT gene on chromosome 4. It exhibits genetic anticipation (earlier onset and greater severity in successive generations, especially on paternal transmission). Striatal (caudate) atrophy is the pathological hallmark.'),
    121: ('B', 'Wernicke encephalopathy is an acute neuropsychiatric syndrome caused by thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency, classically presenting with confusion, ophthalmoplegia/nystagmus, and ataxia. Administration of glucose without thiamine can precipitate or worsen encephalopathy because thiamine is a required cofactor for pyruvate dehydrogenase in glucose metabolism.'),
    122: ('B', 'Korsakoff syndrome is a chronic, largely irreversible amnestic-confabulatory state that follows untreated Wernicke encephalopathy. Neuropathology reveals petechial hemorrhages, gliosis, and atrophy of the mammillary bodies and anterior/dorsomedial nuclei of the thalamus.'),
    123: ('C', 'Delirium is an acute, fluctuating disturbance in attention, awareness, and cognition, typically triggered by underlying systemic medical illness (e.g., infection, metabolic derangement, medications, or postoperative state after fracture). Unlike dementia, delirium is characteristically fluctuating, features prominent inattention, and is reversible with treatment.'),
    124: ('D', 'A focal aware seizure starts in a circumscribed cortical area (e.g., motor cortex) with preserved consciousness and awareness. When motor activity progresses along the motor strip (Jacksonian march) and subsequently generalizes into a bilateral tonic-clonic convulsion with loss of consciousness, it is classified as a focal aware motor seizure evolving to bilateral tonic-clonic.'),
    125: ('C', 'Absence seizures (petit mal) present in childhood with brief (5-10 second) staring spells, unresponsiveness, and minor automatisms (eyelid fluttering) without postictal confusion. The pathognomonic electroencephalogram (EEG) finding is symmetric, generalized 3-Hz spike-and-wave discharges triggered by hyperventilation.'),
    126: ('D', 'Status epilepticus is defined as continuous seizure activity lasting >= 5 minutes or recurrent seizures without full recovery of consciousness. The first-line emergency medication is an IV benzodiazepine (lorazepam 4 mg IV or diazepam 10 mg IV; IM midazolam if no IV access), followed immediately by an IV non-sedating antiepileptic drug.'),
    127: ('B', 'Clinical features that strongly distinguish a generalized epileptic seizure from syncopal episodes include lateral tongue laceration (highly specific for generalized tonic-clonic seizures), prolonged postictal confusion/somnolence, elevated serum prolactin, and sustained rhythmic motor activity > 30 seconds.'),
    128: ('C', 'In adults aged 65 and older presenting with a first unprovoked seizure, cerebrovascular disease (including past ischemic stroke, intracerebral hemorrhage, and vascular white matter lesions) is the single most common identifiable underlying cause.'),
    129: ('C', 'Juvenile Myoclonic Epilepsy (JME / Janz syndrome) characteristically presents in adolescence with bilateral morning myoclonic jerks of the upper limbs, typically triggered by sleep deprivation or alcohol. Valproic acid is the first-line antiepileptic of choice due to its broad-spectrum efficacy against myoclonic, absence, and generalized tonic-clonic seizures.'),
    130: ('D', 'Atonic seizures ("drop attacks") are characterized by a sudden, brief (<2 seconds) complete loss of postural muscle tone, causing the patient to collapse to the ground or experience sudden head drops, often resulting in traumatic facial injuries. They are typically seen in severe childhood epileptic encephalopathies such as Lennox-Gastaut syndrome.'),
    131: ('C', 'Following a second unprovoked seizure occurring > 24 hours after the first, the patient meets diagnostic criteria for epilepsy, and the recurrence risk over the subsequent 5 years exceeds 60-80%. Long-term antiepileptic monotherapy is indicated to prevent future recurrent seizures.'),
    132: ('B', 'Focal impaired awareness seizures arising from the mesial temporal lobe typically begin with an epigastric rising sensation, déjà vu, or olfactory aura, followed by vacant staring, unresponsiveness, oral automatisms (chewing, lip smacking), and manual automatisms (fumbling with clothes), followed by postictal confusion and amnesia.'),
    133: ('B', 'A simple febrile seizure is defined as a generalized seizure lasting < 15 minutes that occurs in a neurologically normal child aged 6 months to 5 years in the setting of fever, without recurrence in 24 hours. Simple febrile seizures carry an excellent prognosis with no increased risk of intellectual disability; management consists of parental reassurance and antipyretics.'),
    134: ('B', 'Mesial temporal sclerosis (hippocampal sclerosis) is the most common identifiable pathology in adult drug-resistant temporal lobe epilepsy. Magnetic resonance imaging demonstrates unilateral hippocampal atrophy with increased T2/FLAIR signal intensity, reflecting pyramidal neuronal loss and dense fibrillary astrogliosis.'),
    135: ('B', 'Benzodiazepines (lorazepam, diazepam, midazolam) bind allosterically to GABAA receptors and enhance the action of GABA by increasing the FREQUENCY of chloride channel opening, leading to membrane hyperpolarization and rapid suppression of epileptiform activity.'),
    136: ('D', 'Both benzodiazepines and ethanol act as central nervous system depressants by potentiating GABAA receptor chloride currents. Concurrent ingestion produces synergistic, supra-additive sedation that can precipitate fatal respiratory depression and coma.'),
    137: ('E', 'Carbamazepine is a first-line agent for focal seizures. Its mechanism of action involves use-dependent / frequency-dependent blockade of voltage-gated neuronal sodium channels, preferentially binding to and prolonging the inactivated state to suppress high-frequency repetitive action potential firing.'),
    138: ('C', 'Carbamazepine carries a black-box warning for severe bone marrow suppression, specifically agranulocytosis (severe absolute neutropenia) and aplastic anemia. Baseline and periodic complete blood count (CBC) monitoring is recommended.'),
    139: ('A', 'Carbamazepine can induce syndrome of inappropriate antidiuretic hormone secretion (SIADH), especially in older adults. It enhances renal collecting duct sensitivity to circulating ADH, producing euvolemic hyponatremia with inappropriately concentrated urine.'),
    140: ('D', 'Valproic acid has a broad mechanism of action: it blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, blocks low-threshold T-type calcium channels in the thalamus, and increases inhibitory GABA transmission by inhibiting GABA transaminase and stimulating glutamate decarboxylase.'),
    141: ('E', 'Valproic acid carries black-box warnings for idiosyncratic fulminant hepatotoxicity (most common in children < 2 years and patients with underlying mitochondrial disorders) and acute necrotizing pancreatitis, which can develop rapidly regardless of treatment duration.'),
    142: ('C', 'Valproic acid is the most teratogenic antiepileptic drug, carrying a 1-2% risk of neural tube defects (spina bifida, anencephaly) due to folate metabolism interference, as well as significant risks of major congenital malformations and impaired cognitive development; it is avoided in women of childbearing potential.'),
    143: ('A', 'Phenytoin suppresses focal and generalized tonic-clonic seizures by use-dependent blockade of voltage-gated neuronal sodium channels, binding selectively to the inactivated conformation to prevent repetitive sustained axonal firing.'),
    144: ('B', 'Chronic phenytoin therapy is characteristically associated with gingival hyperplasia (induced by PDGF-stimulated gingival fibroblast proliferation) and hirsutism. Other chronic side effects include coarsening of facial features, peripheral neuropathy, and megaloblastic anemia due to folate malabsorption.'),
    145: ('E', 'Phenytoin exhibits nonlinear, saturable (Michaelis-Menten / zero-order) elimination kinetics within the therapeutic range. Once hepatic metabolizing enzymes (CYP2C9) are saturated, small incremental dosage increases lead to disproportionately large elevations in serum drug levels, precipitating acute neurotoxicity (nystagmus, ataxia, diplopia).'),
    146: ('D', 'Phenytoin is a potent inducer of hepatic cytochrome P450 enzymes (particularly CYP3A4). It significantly accelerates the metabolic clearance of estrogen and progestin components of oral contraceptives, leading to subtherapeutic hormone levels and contraceptive failure.'),
    147: ('B', 'Lamotrigine suppresses seizures by blocking voltage-gated neuronal sodium channels and stabilizing presynaptic neuronal membranes, which inhibits the downstream vesicular release of excitatory amino acid neurotransmitters (glutamate and aspartate).'),
    148: ('E', 'Lamotrigine carries a black-box warning for severe cutaneous adverse reactions, including Stevens-Johnson syndrome (SJS) and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN). The risk is strongly correlated with rapid initial dose titration and concurrent use of valproic acid (which inhibits lamotrigine glucuronidation).'),
    149: ('B', 'Ethosuximide is the drug of choice for absence seizures. It acts selectively by blocking low-threshold T-type voltage-gated calcium channels in thalamic relay neurons, thereby disrupting the pacemaker current responsible for generating the synchronous 3-Hz spike-and-wave discharge.'),
    150: ('C', 'Valproic acid is correctly paired with acute pancreatitis and hepatotoxicity. Phenytoin causes gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism (not agranulocytosis); carbamazepine causes agranulocytosis, aplastic anemia, and SIADH (not gingival hyperplasia); lamotrigine causes Stevens-Johnson syndrome.')
}
